{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778942201,"nonce":"s9NNCYZZI/WJRd5/W4D4UKrK7ks=","message":"## Implementation design v0: git-bug adapter, mirror, and conflict handling\n\nThis issue should implement git-bug as a compatibility adapter around the ledger. It should not keep or reintroduce git-bug as the normal issue read/write path.\n\n### Module boundary\n\nSuggested modules under `src/ledger/`:\n\n- `git-bug-client.ts`: small subprocess wrapper around git-bug/git commands, with timeouts and typed results.\n- `git-bug-importer.ts`: maps git-bug JSON into ledger commands.\n- `git-bug-exporter.ts`: consumes ledger outbox rows and writes git-bug-compatible changes.\n- `git-bug-labels.ts`: parse/format namespaced labels and legacy labels.\n- `sync-runner.ts`: queue/job orchestration, retry, locking policy, status updates.\n- `reconcile.ts`: remote cursor comparison and conflict creation.\n\nDo not extend `IssueScanner` for canonical behavior. `IssueScanner` can remain as legacy scanner and reconciliation comparison input.\n\n### Git-bug client\n\nThe client should be explicit and testable:\n\n- `listIssues(repoRoot): GitBugSummary[]` wraps `git-bug bug --format=json`.\n- `getIssue(repoRoot, ref): GitBugDetail` wraps `git-bug bug show \u003cref\u003e --format=json`.\n- `createIssue(repoRoot, input)` creates a git-bug issue and returns external IDs if possible.\n- `setStatus(repoRoot, ref, status)` uses `git-bug bug status close/open`.\n- `addComment(repoRoot, ref, body)` uses non-interactive comment creation.\n- `addLabel(repoRoot, ref, label)` and `removeLabel(repoRoot, ref, label)`.\n- `hasGitBugRefs(repoRoot)` keeps existing behavior for repos with no git-bug data.\n\nEvery command should have a timeout and return structured `{ ok, stdout, stderr, error, exitCode }`, not just boolean/null. Lock failures should be classified as retryable if detectable from stderr.\n\nNever delete git-bug lock files in this path.\n\n### Import algorithm\n\nImport is project/check-out scoped but writes canonical project IDs.\n\nRecommended flow:\n\n1. Enumerate ledger projects/checkouts that have a valid root and git-bug refs.\n2. For each checkout, list git-bug issue summaries.\n3. For each issue, fetch detail JSON. Do not rely on summary-only rows for body/comments.\n4. Canonicalize the remote detail payload into a stable hash. Store that hash/version in `ledger_external_refs`.\n5. Resolve or create a ledger issue via aliases:\n   - `git-bug:\u003cproject_id\u003e:\u003chuman_id\u003e`;\n   - full git-bug ID if available;\n   - short human ID as a display alias scoped to the project.\n6. Preserve title, body, status, labels, comments, author display, create/update times when available.\n7. Treat git-bug's first comment/description convention carefully: body goes to `ledger_issues.body`; subsequent comments go to `ledger_comments`.\n8. Upsert comments by external comment ID when available. If git-bug lacks stable comment IDs, use an imported comment fingerprint of project + issue external ID + author + timestamp/body hash.\n9. Run relationships in a second pass after all issues are present so parent/child labels can resolve targets.\n10. Import should write `source='git_bug'` or `source='import'` events but should not enqueue export outbox rows for unchanged imported data.\n\nImport must be idempotent. Re-running import on the same git-bug state should produce no duplicate issues, comments, labels, aliases, relationships, conflicts, or outbox work.\n\n### Label mapping\n\nPreserve raw labels in `ledger_labels`. Also parse stable semantics where labels are a reasonable representation.\n\nLegacy labels:\n\n- `parent:\u003cid\u003e` -\u003e `parent_child` relationship.\n- `merged` -\u003e closed issue resolution can become `merged` when status is closed and no more specific resolution exists.\n\nNamespaced labels to export/import:\n\n- `mandible:kind:\u003ckind\u003e`\n- `mandible:priority:\u003cpriority\u003e`\n- `mandible:parent:\u003cid\u003e`\n- `mandible:blocked-by:\u003cid\u003e`\n- `mandible:blocks:\u003cid\u003e`\n- `mandible:related:\u003cid\u003e`\n- `mandible:duplicate-of:\u003cid\u003e`\n- `mandible:references:\u003cid\u003e`\n\nKeep relationship labels scoped to display aliases that remain meaningful outside Mandible. On import, resolve target aliases through the ledger resolver; if unresolved, store a derived relationship candidate in metadata or leave a conflict/warning rather than creating a dangling canonical edge.\n\nAvoid exporting volatile runtime state as labels.\n\n### Export algorithm\n\nExport consumes `ledger_outbox` rather than diffing whole issues.\n\nRecommended flow:\n\n1. Select pending outbox rows for adapter `git_bug` grouped by project/check-out.\n2. Before applying a row, fetch current remote detail for the target issue when an external issue already exists.\n3. Compare current remote hash with `ledger_external_refs.remote_hash` / last seen cursor.\n4. If remote changed externally, create `ledger_conflicts`, mark outbox row `conflicted`, and do not overwrite silently.\n5. If safe, apply the minimal git-bug operation: create issue, add comment, close/reopen, add/remove label.\n6. Fetch detail after mutation and update `ledger_external_refs` with new remote hash/version and timestamps.\n7. Mark outbox row `applied` or retryable `failed` with structured error.\n\nFor creation, the exporter should write human-readable issue body and labels. If later git-bug cannot return generated IDs cleanly from create output, immediately re-list/filter by title/body fingerprint to identify the created issue; tests should pin the chosen strategy.\n\n### Conflict model\n\nCreate field-level conflicts for non-append-only divergence:\n\n- title;\n- body/description;\n- status/resolution;\n- labels when both sides changed since the last cursor;\n- relationship labels that resolve to different canonical targets.\n\nComments are append-oriented. External-only comments should usually import as new comments instead of conflicting. Comment edits/deletions, if git-bug exposes them, can become conflicts or additional imported comments depending on adapter fidelity.\n\nConflict records must contain:\n\n- adapter/project/entity;\n- field;\n- base value if known;\n- local value;\n- remote value;\n- remote cursor/hash;\n- status.\n\nThe UI issue will add resolution actions, but this adapter issue must create records that make those actions possible.\n\n### Sync jobs and retry policy\n\nUse serialized jobs per project/check-out so concurrent git-bug commands do not recreate lock contention. A stuck lock should mark the job failed/retryable and leave normal ledger reads/writes unaffected.\n\nRecommended policy:\n\n- one active git-bug sync per checkout root;\n- retry retryable failures with backoff;\n- do not retry validation/conflict failures automatically;\n- expose last error/status/counts in job rows;\n- support manual retry from API/UI later.\n\n### Structured metadata blocks\n\nDo not dump the full ledger into git-bug by default. If structured metadata is needed for round-trip, use a clearly namespaced hidden block or trailer with versioning, for example:\n\n```markdown\n\u003c!-- mandible-ledger:v1\n{ \"issue_id\": \"iss_...\", \"project_id\": \"prj_...\", \"aliases\": [...] }\n--\u003e\n```\n\nUse this sparingly. Prefer labels for durable human-readable facts and reserve structured blocks for data required for reconstruction but not naturally label-shaped.\n\n### Tests\n\nAdapter tests should use fixtures first and temp repos where stable.\n\nRequired tests:\n\n- parse current git-bug summary/detail JSON, including missing optional fields;\n- first-comment body mapping and subsequent comment mapping;\n- idempotent import on repeated runs;\n- parent/child label two-pass import;\n- namespaced label parse/format round trip;\n- outbox export applies the expected minimal git-bug operation through a fake client;\n- remote hash/cursor mismatch creates conflict instead of overwrite;\n- lock-like git-bug failure marks job retryable and does not mutate canonical issue state;\n- exported core fields can be imported back without losing title/body/status/labels/comments/basic relationships.\n\n### Performance expectations\n\nImport may be batch/background work, but it should not block the UI thread. Avoid `spawnSync` in request handlers. Adapter code may run controlled subprocesses in a background job path with concurrency limited per checkout.\n\n### Done check\n\nAfter this issue, the ledger should be able to mirror core issue state to/from git-bug with explicit cursors and conflicts. It is acceptable if route reads/writes still use the old scanner until the migration gate issue switches them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}